I found the current CAP/DA clinical policy, Policy 3K-2, effective Sept. 1, 2025, and checked amendments. The amendments lookup returns 3K-2 itself as the authoritative current document, with no separate amending notice listed. I have enough to answer the general eligibility question from the policy’s eligibility and covered-criteria sections.## General eligibility criteria for CAP/DA under NC Medicaid

Under current NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, a person generally must meet **both Medicaid eligibility requirements and CAP/DA program-specific criteria** to receive CAP/DA services. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) (effective Sept. 1, 2025; amendments check indicates this is the authoritative current document).

### 1. The person must be enrolled in NC Medicaid
The policy states the beneficiary must be enrolled in the NC Medicaid Program, and some eligibility categories may still have service restrictions that make a person ineligible for this service. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 2.1.1.

### 2. The person must meet CAP/DA waiver eligibility requirements
The policy’s Medicaid eligibility subsection says the beneficiary must:

- be enrolled in **Medicaid Direct (fee-for-service)**;
- be determined to require an **institutional level of care** under the NC State Medicaid Plan;
- be a member of a **target group** documented in the approved waiver application;
- meet applicable Medicaid eligibility criteria referenced in the policy;
- **choose the waiver** rather than institutional care;
- be assigned a **CAP/DA waiver slot**;
- be able to have health, safety, and well-being maintained in the **primary residence or another approved location** with formal and informal supports; and
- be **age 18 or older**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), subsection titled “a. Medicaid.”

### 3. The person must meet the specific covered criteria for CAP/DA
The policy further says Medicaid covers CAP/DA when the beneficiary:

- meets the required **HCBS nursing facility level of care**;
- has a completed CAP assessment showing a **reasonable indication of need** for at least one CAP/DA waiver service within **30 calendar days** of evaluation;
- has a **signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis to prevent institutionalization, or a short-term authorized equipment/modification/technology plan to help the person remain in or return to the home;
- can maintain health, safety, and well-being at home or an approved setting with formal and informal supports;
- has paid care needs that can be maintained within the **average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
- has a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- has an **emergency back-up and disaster plan**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.2.1.

## In short
A person is generally eligible for CAP/DA if they are:

- on NC Medicaid,
- **18 or older**,
- in **Medicaid Direct**,
- eligible for the CAP/DA waiver and assigned a **waiver slot**,
- in need of a **nursing-facility level of care**,
- able to be safely served in the community with supports,
- shown by assessment to need at least one waiver service,
- and covered by an approved **service plan** designed to avoid institutionalization.

