I found the current CAP/DA policy and confirmed via the amendments tool that the 2025-09-01 CAP/DA document is the authoritative current source. I also pulled the appendix section on “Equipment, Modification and Technology,” plus the related documentation and service-plan sections. Next I’ll answer using only the text I could verify, noting where the policy text appears truncated or broader than home modifications specifically.## CAP/DA home modifications: requirements and MDT documentation

Under current **CAP/DA Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2** (effective **Sept. 1, 2025**), home modifications fall within the waiver service for **“Equipment, Modification and Technology.”** I checked amendments for this document and the policy itself is the current authoritative source, with no separate amending notice returned. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 1) What kinds of home modifications CAP/DA allows

The policy says home modifications may be made to:

- the beneficiary’s **primary private residence** if owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a **rented residence** if the modifications are **portable**; or
- a rented residence if the owner allows the portable modification. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

Examples of covered home modifications listed in the policy include:

- installation, maintenance, and repair of **ramps**, **grab bars**, **safety rails**, and **handrails**;
- **widening doorways or passages** for wheelchair or walker access;
- **emergency egress** when medically necessary because of the responsible party’s physical limitations;
- **bathroom modifications** to improve accessibility, including toilets, showers/tubs, hand-held showers, sinks, faucet controls, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- **bedroom modifications** to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- **kitchen modifications** for accessibility, including cabinets, sinks, faucet controls, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- **floor coverings** for ease of ambulation;
- **hydraulic, manual, or electronic lifts**, including portable lift systems used primarily inside the home;
- **non-skid surfaces**;
- **door handle replacements** and other **door modifications**;
- **smart home devices** such as smart bulbs, switches, entryway controls, clocks, and thermostats, but only when the beneficiary **lives alone** and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan;
- certain **air filtration systems and filters** when the policy’s medical criteria are met; and
- a portable back-up generator for a ventilator in limited circumstances. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy also includes related costs/services tied to approved modifications, such as:

- **technical assistance in device selection**;
- **training** in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- **purchase costs**, including necessary permits, inspections, taxes, and delivery charges;
- **installation**;
- **assessment of the modification** by the case manager and applicable inspectors to verify safety and ability to meet the beneficiary’s needs; and
- **repair**, if repair costs do not exceed replacement cost and the item is not under warranty. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 2) Key limits/exclusions relevant to home modifications

The policy excludes items/services that are:

- **recreational in nature**;
- of **general utility to a non-disabled beneficiary**;
- covered under the **Home Health Final Rule**;
- covered through the Medicaid State Plan as **DME, orthotics, prosthetics, or home health supplies**;
- service agreements or maintenance contracts not related to the approved service;
- warranties; and
- certain expressly excluded items such as swimming pool/spa equipment and some security/monitoring items. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

So, for a home modification to qualify under CAP/DA, the policy text supports that it must be tied to the beneficiary’s disability-related need and not just be a general home improvement or an item otherwise covered elsewhere.

### 3) What documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

The clearest verified documentation requirements in the CAP/DA policy are these:

- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the **case manager** identifying the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, **when applicable**;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification requested; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the equipment/supply/adaptation/modification was provided and the cost, including related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

Another CAP/DA limits section adds one more requirement for requests in this area:

- the request must indicate **the estimated life of the equipment** and **the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 4) What should be in the service plan

The person-centered service plan must, at minimum:

- summarize evaluation/assessment information showing strengths and needs;
- outline person-centered goals and objectives based on assessed needs; and
- develop a comprehensive list of services, supplies, and DME, documenting the authorized provider, amount, frequency, and duration. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

That means if a home modification is being requested or authorized, the assessment findings and the modification should align with the person-centered service plan.

## Short practical summary

For **CAP/DA home modifications**, the policy supports coverage for disability-related accessibility changes to the beneficiary’s primary home, including items like ramps, grab bars, widened doorways, bathroom/bedroom/kitchen accessibility changes, lifts, non-skid surfaces, and some smart-home and filtration items when specific criteria are met. The modification cannot be merely recreational, of general utility, or otherwise covered through another Medicaid benefit. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The **MDT/case-management documentation** must include at least:

1. a **comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** identifying the modification need;  
2. a **physician’s order**, when applicable;  
3. an **appropriate professional recommendation** describing the beneficiary’s need for the requested modification; and  
4. a **supplier invoice** documenting provision date and cost. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

